Schema-driven development starts with accepted values and declared outcomes. In this repo, schemas are executable boundaries. They are not separate descriptions of TypeScript interfaces.

[`packages/core/src/join-interest-contract.ts`](https://github.com/joelhooks/rat-stack/blob/main/packages/core/src/join-interest-contract.ts) defines `joinInterest`:

- Contact consent is `Schema.Literal(true)`.
- Answers are optional bounded strings.
- The ticket and agent reference are bounded nonempty strings.
- Sharing consent is optional.

Contact consent does not imply sharing consent.

[`AbuseVerdictSchema`](https://github.com/joelhooks/rat-stack/blob/main/packages/core/src/abuse-score.ts) defines:

- A boolean hold.
- A finite score between 0 and 1.
- An array of signal names.

The TypeScript `AbuseVerdict` type comes from `typeof AbuseVerdictSchema.Type`.

## Decode before dispatch

Effect's [`Schema.decodeUnknownEffect`](https://github.com/Effect-TS/effect/blob/effect%404.0.0/packages/effect/src/Schema.ts) decodes unknown input or fails with a schema error. Our [code-mode invoker](https://github.com/joelhooks/rat-stack/blob/main/packages/capability/src/to-code-mode.ts) decodes input before calling the handler. It then encodes the declared output or failure.

A domain refusal is typed too. [`MaskedInterestReplay`](https://github.com/joelhooks/rat-stack/blob/main/packages/core/src/masked-interest-replay.ts) is a Schema tagged error. The [handler](https://github.com/joelhooks/rat-stack/blob/main/packages/core/src/join-interest.ts) returns it when someone tries to replay a redacted card.

That refusal differs from a malformed request's [empty HTTP 400](/lore/openapi-describes-the-refusal-too).

## Generate cases, then state the rule

The [intake property tests](https://github.com/joelhooks/rat-stack/blob/main/packages/core/test/join-interest.test.ts) use `Arbitrary.schema(Scenario)`. Cases combine availability, sharing consent, ticket validity, and holds. Another property generates response histories.

The schema supplies cases. The tests still state the behavior those cases must satisfy.

See [property-based testing](/lore/property-based-testing) and [model-based testing](/lore/model-based-testing).

See [how we do it with Effect](/lore/how-we-do-it-with-effect) for the whole path.
